TP.register({id:"lp6-g5",lp:6,grade:"5",mode:"curriculum",checks:5,nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="Three Hundred and Nine";const FLUENCY="Yara climbed the east tower stairs with the logbook under her arm and the wind pushing at her back. From the top she could see the whole lantern rope at once, a long sagging line of paper and light running the width of the river.\n\nHer job was the count. Three hundred and twelve lanterns had gone up that afternoon, and every one of them had to come down again in the morning. A lantern left on the rope through a week of rain turns to pulp, and the hook it hangs on rusts through by spring.\n\nShe started at the west end and worked her eye along, marking tens with a pencil stroke. Below her the walkers crossed in a slow line, and their voices came up thin and far away, the way voices do over water.\n\nAt three hundred and nine she stopped and went back. She counted again, more slowly, and got the same number. Somewhere out over the middle of the river, three lanterns had come loose, and three hooks were hanging empty in the dark.";const LIST_TITLE="What the List Said";const LIST=["Nilay kept his goal list folded inside the back cover of his math notebook, where nobody would look. He had written it in October, four lines, and he had not read it since.","His sister Amara found it in February, and not on purpose. The notebook fell open on the kitchen table and there it was.","“You wrote these down?” she said. She did not laugh. That surprised him more than anything else about the whole afternoon.","“It was a school thing,” Nilay said, which was only half true. The school had handed out cards. He had thrown the card away and written his own list that night instead.","Amara read the four lines twice. Then she took a pencil and put a small check beside the second one, the one about talking to people he did not know yet. “That one is done,” she said. “You just have not noticed.”","Nilay took the notebook back. He looked at the check for a long time, and he did not erase it."].join("\n\n");const WATER_TITLE="Lights on the Water";const WATER=["The last of the walkers went home at ten, and the bridge belonged to the crew again. Omar sat down on the cold stone with his back against the rail and let his legs hang out over the water.","The rope above him was a long sentence of light, every lantern a word he could not read. Below, the river had copied it badly. The reflection broke and healed and broke again, a ribbon of gold pulling apart in the current.","Mei came and sat down next to him without a word. Her breath went up in small clouds. Somewhere behind them a door closed, and the sound traveled out over the water and came back thinner, as if the river had borrowed it and given most of it away.","“Three hundred and nine,” Omar said at last.","“I know,” said Mei. “We will find them in the morning.”","The cold crept up through the stone and into them, patient as a cat, and still neither of them moved."].join("\n\n");const LY_TITLE="Reading a Light Year";const LY=["A light year sounds like a measure of time, but it is a measure of distance. It is the distance light travels in one year, which comes to about 5.9 trillion miles.","Astronomers use it because the numbers in space get unwieldy fast. Writing the distance to a nearby star in miles takes a string of digits nobody can hold in their head. Writing the same distance as 4.2 light years is something a person can picture.","The unit carries a second piece of information for free. A star 4.2 light years away is also a star whose light took 4.2 years to arrive. Look at a star 600 light years out and you are seeing light that left before your great-great-grandparents were born. The farther away an object sits, the deeper into the past you are looking."].join("\n\n");const S1={title:"The Glow Over Town",src:"from <i>The Vanishing Sky</i>, a book by Idris Falk",body:"Most people who live in a city or a town have never seen the Milky Way. The reason is not the air and it is not the stars. It is the light we put up ourselves. Streetlights, parking lots, and signs throw light sideways and upward as well as down. That stray light scatters off dust and water droplets in the air and turns the whole sky a faint orange-gray. Astronomers call this skyglow. Against a glowing sky, only the brightest few dozen stars still show."};const S2={title:"Shielding the Light",src:"“Getting the Stars Back,” a magazine article by Priya Raman",body:"A town does not have to go dark to get its stars back. Most of the fix is the shape of the fixture. A shielded lamp has a solid cap above the bulb, so the light goes down onto the sidewalk where it is wanted instead of out into the sky. Warmer, more yellow light scatters less in the air than cool blue-white light does. Many towns also put lights on timers, cutting the hours a parking lot stands lit at three in the morning. Towns that have made these changes report that the ground is just as bright to walk on, and the sky overhead is darker."};const S3={title:"Who Else Is Out There",src:"“Night Work,” a science website page by Caleb Mwangi",body:"People are not the only ones affected. Many birds migrate at night and steer partly by the stars, and a brightly lit town can pull them off course. Moths and other night insects circle lit bulbs until they are worn out, which keeps them away from the plants they would otherwise pollinate. Sea turtle hatchlings crawl toward the brightest horizon, which for millions of years meant the open water. Near a lit beach road, they crawl the wrong way."};const TALLY_READ=["A Note on the Tally.","The count is done from the east tower, because that is the only place you can see the whole rope at once. The counter marks tens with a pencil stroke and counts the strokes at the end.","Marking tens matters. Counting one by one up past three hundred, a person loses the place about half the time. Two counters work on separate evenings and compare their numbers, and if the two disagree, both of them count again the next night."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["reflection","illuminate","observatory","unpredictable","reconsider","telescopic","misdirection","immeasurable","transparent","astronomer","glimberous","disprantic"];const SKY_BOX=["Skyglow is the orange-gray haze over a town, made by light thrown sideways and upward.","Streetlights, parking lots, and signs are the main sources.","A shielded lamp has a solid cap above the bulb, so the light goes down instead of up.","Warmer, more yellow light scatters less in the air than cool blue-white light.","Timers cut the hours a lot stands lit in the middle of the night.","Towns that shield their lights say the ground is just as bright and the sky is darker.","Night-migrating birds steer partly by the stars, and bright towns pull them off course."];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:8px 18px;margin:14px auto 0;max-width:860px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const sourceBlock=(...list)=>`<div class="texts">${list.map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Source ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t.src}</p><p>${t.body}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const textBlock=(...list)=>`<div class="texts">${list.map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Text ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p>${t.body}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const pills=items=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${items.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.2rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;function areaModel(){const ox=104,oy=42,S=252,cw=S/4,rh=S/3,W=ox+S+76,H=oy+S+50;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A one meter by one meter square cut into 4 columns and 3 rows, making 12 equal parts. The block that is 3 columns wide and 2 rows tall is shaded.">`;for(let r=0;r<3;r++)for(let c=0;c<4;c++)s+=`<rect x="${ox+c*cw}" y="${oy+r*rh}" width="${cw}" height="${rh}" fill="${r<2&&c<3?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;const t=(x,y,txt,an)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${an||"middle"}" font-size="19" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${txt}</text>`;s+=`<line x1="${ox}" y1="${oy-12}" x2="${ox+3*cw}" y2="${oy-12}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`+t(ox+1.5*cw,oy-20,"3/4 m");s+=`<line x1="${ox-14}" y1="${oy}" x2="${ox-14}" y2="${oy+2*rh}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`+t(ox-22,oy+rh+6,"2/3 m","end");s+=t(ox+S/2,oy+S+32,"1 m")+t(ox+S+12,oy+S/2+6,"1 m","start");return s+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Tower Count",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:110,dir:"Read the story out loud at your normal speed. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLUENCY,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute, mark the last word read, and count errors on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Grade 5 winter benchmark (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017): 50th percentile 133 words correct per minute, 25th percentile 109, 10th percentile 84.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the pauses at the commas in the long first sentence, and for a drop in pace at “She counted again, more slowly.”"]}]},{id:2,title:"Inside the Brackets",short:"Brackets",parts:[{std:["5.OA.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Solve each one on your paper. Show the step inside the brackets first, then hold your paper up.",desc:"using parentheses and brackets in expressions and finding their value",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew works out its orders on paper before it calls them in. Find the value of each expression.</p>`,qs:["6 × (9 + 4) = ?","(24 ÷ 4) + (5 × 3) = ?","2 × [15 − (3 × 4)] = ?","45 − [(6 + 3) × 4] = ?","Write an expression with parentheses for this: add 8 and 7, then multiply the sum by 6. Then find its value.","Does 3 × (10 − 4) equal 3 × 10 − 4? Find both values and say which one is greater."],key:["78. Inside first: 9 + 4 = 13, then 6 × 13 = 78. Partial: 58 comes from multiplying before adding and does not count. (5.OA.A.1)","21. 24 ÷ 4 = 6 and 5 × 3 = 15, then 6 + 15 = 21. Partial: one of the two parentheses worked out is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","6. Innermost first: 3 × 4 = 12. Then 15 − 12 = 3, and 2 × 3 = 6. Partial: 3 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","9. Innermost first: 6 + 3 = 9. Then 9 × 4 = 36, and 45 − 36 = 9. Partial: 36 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","6 × (8 + 7), which equals 6 × 15 = 90. Accept (8 + 7) × 6. Partial: 90 given with no expression written is approaching. (5.OA.A.1, writing an expression with parentheses)","No, they are not equal. 3 × (10 − 4) = 3 × 6 = 18, and 3 × 10 − 4 = 30 − 4 = 26. The second one, 3 × 10 − 4, is greater. Partial: saying they are not equal with only one value worked out is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"What the List Said",short:"Goal List",parts:[{std:["RL.5.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the story that prove each answer.",desc:"quoting a story accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(LIST,LIST_TITLE,true),qs:["Where did Nilay keep his goal list? Read me the exact words.","When did he write the list, and how many lines were on it? Read me the words that tell you.","The story never says Nilay was embarrassed about the list. Read me words that show it anyway.","Did the check mark mean something to Nilay? Read me the exact words that prove your answer.","Why does the story say his answer was only half true? Read me the words that explain it."],key:["Quotes: “folded inside the back cover of his math notebook, where nobody would look.” The student should read the words as written, not retell them loosely. (RL.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “He had written it in October, four lines, and he had not read it since.” (RL.5.1, explicit)","Quotes either “where nobody would look” or “‘It was a school thing,’ Nilay said, which was only half true.” Hiding the list and playing it down are the evidence. (RL.5.1, inference backed by a quote)","Yes. Quotes: “He looked at the check for a long time, and he did not erase it.” (RL.5.1, inference backed by a quote)","Quotes: “The school had handed out cards. He had thrown the card away and written his own list that night instead.” The school started it, but the list was his own. (RL.5.1, explicit)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Ten Times and One Tenth",short:"Place Value",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Answer out loud. Explain how you know.",desc:"a digit worth 10 times the place to its right and 1/10 of the place to its left",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew counts lanterns, meters of rope, and liters of oil, so the same digit turns up in different places all night.</p>`,fig:()=>`<p class="huge" style="margin:0">7,700</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">lantern hooks counted along the whole river walk</p>`,qs:["In 7,700, how does the 7 in the thousands place compare to the 7 in the hundreds place?","In 3,300, the 3 in the hundreds place is worth what part of the 3 in the thousands place?","In 0.88, how does the 8 in the tenths place compare to the 8 in the hundredths place?","A 6 in the tens place is worth 60. In which place would a 6 be worth one tenth of that, and what is it worth there?","Use place value to explain why 4,000 ÷ 400 = 10.","In 55.5, compare each 5 to the 5 just to its right."],key:["It is worth 10 times as much. The 7 in the thousands place is 7,000 and the 7 in the hundreds place is 700, and 7,000 is 10 times 700. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","One tenth of it. 300 is 1/10 of 3,000. Partial: 10 times as much reverses the comparison and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","It is worth 10 times as much. The 8 in the tenths place is 0.8 and the 8 in the hundredths place is 0.08, and 0.8 is 10 times 0.08. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The ones place, where a 6 is worth 6. 6 is one tenth of 60. Partial: naming the place with no value given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The 4 in the thousands place is worth 10 times the 4 in the hundreds place, so 4,000 is 10 groups of 400. That is why the quotient is 10. Partial: working the division out with no place value reason is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The 5 in the tens place is 50 and the 5 in the ones place is 5, so 50 is 10 times 5. The 5 in the ones place is 5 and the 5 in the tenths place is 0.5, so 5 is 10 times 0.5. Each 5 is worth 10 times the 5 one place to its right. Partial: one of the two comparisons given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Reading a Light Year",short:"Light Year",parts:[{std:["RI.5.1"],dur:120,dir:"Read the article. Then answer out loud with the exact words from the article.",desc:"quoting an informational text accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(LY,LY_TITLE,true),qs:["A light year measures what? Read me the exact words from the article.","Why do astronomers use light years instead of miles? Read me the sentence that answers this.","How long ago did the light from a star 600 light years away leave that star? Read me the words that back up your answer.","A telescope that can see farther out is also a telescope that can see further back in time. Read me the sentence from the article that supports that."],key:["Distance, not time. Quotes: “It is the distance light travels in one year, which comes to about 5.9 trillion miles.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","Quotes: “Writing the distance to a nearby star in miles takes a string of digits nobody can hold in their head.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","600 years ago. Quotes: “A star 4.2 light years away is also a star whose light took 4.2 years to arrive,” or “you are seeing light that left before your great-great-grandparents were born.” (RI.5.1, inference backed by a quote)","Quotes: “The farther away an object sits, the deeper into the past you are looking.” (RI.5.1, inference backed by a quote)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Thousandths of a Meter",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],dur:140,dir:"Say numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write numbers 3 through 6 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading, writing, and comparing decimals to thousandths",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The rope is measured to the nearest thousandth of a meter. So every number in the logbook has three places after the decimal point.</p>`,qs:["Read this number out loud: 2.405","Say 0.067 in words.","Write this as a decimal number: three and nineteen thousandths","Write 4.236 in expanded form.","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 0.6 ____ 0.58","Put these in order from least to greatest: 0.7, 0.07, 0.77, 0.707"],key:["Two and four hundred five thousandths. “Two point four zero five” on its own does not meet the standard, which asks for the number name. Partial: two point four zero five is approaching, since the place value name is what is asked. (5.NBT.A.3)","Sixty-seven thousandths. Partial: zero point zero six seven is approaching, since the place value name is what is asked. (5.NBT.A.3)","3.019. The nineteen thousandths fills the hundredths and thousandths places, so a zero holds the tenths place. Partial: 3.19 leaves no zero holding the tenths place and does not count. (5.NBT.A.3)","4 + 0.2 + 0.03 + 0.006. Accept (4 × 1) + (2 × 1/10) + (3 × 1/100) + (6 × 1/1000). Partial: three of the four places written is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3, expanded form)","0.6 &gt; 0.58. Line up the places: 0.600 against 0.580, and 6 tenths beats 5 tenths. Or: compare 600 thousandths against 580 thousandths. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3)","0.07, 0.7, 0.707, 0.77. Written to three places they are 0.070, 0.700, 0.707, 0.770. Partial: three of the four in order is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Crate Label Words",short:"Crate Words",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:70,dir:"Read each word out loud. The last two are made-up words. Just sound them out.",desc:"reading long words using prefixes, suffixes, roots, and syllable chunks",key:["Read each word in order. glimberous and disprantic are made-up words, so the student has to use chunks and word parts instead of memory: glim-ber-ous, dis-pran-tic.","Watch for prefixes (re-, un-, pre-, mis-, im-, trans-, dis-), suffixes (-tion, -ate, -ory, -able, -ic, -ent, -er, -ous), and roots (flect for bend, lumin for light, serv for watch, dict for say, tele and scope for far and see, measur, par for appear, astro for star).","Watch whether the student breaks a long word into syllable chunks (ob-ser-va-tor-y, un-pre-dict-a-ble, im-meas-ur-a-ble, as-tron-o-mer) or guesses from the first few letters."]}]},{id:8,title:"Share the Supplies",short:"Shares",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.3"],dur:125,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"a fraction as division, with sharing problems that give fraction and mixed number answers",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Everything on the bridge gets split into equal shares. Solve these.</p>`,qs:["5 sheets of lantern paper are shared equally among 8 students. How much of a sheet does each student get?","Write 29 ÷ 8 as a fraction and as a mixed number.","A 22 kilogram box of candles is split equally among 4 crews. How many kilograms does each crew get, and between which two whole numbers is your answer?","3 liters of lamp oil are poured equally into 4 lanterns. How much goes in each one? Tell how you know the answer is less than 1 liter.","7 meters of ribbon are cut into 10 equal pieces. How long is each piece? Write it as a fraction and as a decimal."],key:["5/8 of a sheet. 5 ÷ 8 = 5/8. Partial: 8/5 reverses the share and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","29/8 and 3 5/8, because 8 × 3 = 24 with 5 left over. Partial: one of the two forms given is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","22/4 kilograms, which is 11/2 or 5 1/2 kilograms. It lies between 5 and 6. Partial: 22/4 right with no pair of whole numbers named is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","3/4 of a liter. There are fewer liters than lanterns, so each lantern has to get less than a whole liter. Partial: 3/4 right with no reason that it is less than 1 is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","7/10 of a meter, which is 0.7 meter. Partial: one of the fraction and the decimal given is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)"]}]},{id:9,title:"A Sentence of Light",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.5.4"],dur:120,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"explaining similes, metaphors, and words as they are used in a story",html:()=>F.passage(WATER,WATER_TITLE,true),qs:["“The rope above him was a long sentence of light, every lantern a word he could not read.” Simile or metaphor? How do you know, and what does it tell you about how the lanterns looked?","“a ribbon of gold pulling apart in the current.” What is being compared to a ribbon, and what does the author mean?","Find the simile that tells how the cold moved. What does it mean?","“as if the river had borrowed it and given most of it away.” What really happened to the sound?","What does the word copied mean in “the river had copied it badly”?"],key:["A metaphor, because it says the rope was a sentence with no like or as. The lanterns hung in a line the way words sit in a sentence, each one separate but part of one long thing, and Omar could see the pattern without being able to say what it meant. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","The reflection of the lantern light on the river. A ribbon is long, thin, and here gold-colored, and the current keeps tearing it and letting it close again. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","“patient as a cat.” The cold came on slowly and steadily, in no hurry, and it did not give up. (RL.5.4, simile)","The sound of the closing door traveled out over the water and came back much fainter. The author describes that fading as the river borrowing the sound and keeping most of it. (RL.5.4, figurative language)","Reflected. The river showed an image of the lantern rope, but a broken and shifting one rather than a clean picture, which is why the story says it copied it badly. (RL.5.4, meaning of a word as used in the text)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Fractions of the Walk",short:"Multiply",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.4"],dur:160,dir:"Solve on your paper. For number 6, use the picture. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"multiplying a whole number by a fraction and a fraction by a fraction, with an area model",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Every job on the bridge is measured in parts of the whole. Work these out.</p>`,qs:["3/5 × 8 = ?","2/3 × 3/4 = ?","5/6 × 42 = ?","The crew folded 36 lanterns. 3/4 of them were hung over the river. How many were hung over the river?","7/8 × 2/3 = ?","Look at the picture. The big square is one square meter of the deck. The shaded patch is 3/4 of a meter long and 2/3 of a meter wide. Write a multiplication equation for the area of the patch and give the answer."],fig2:()=>F.row([areaModel(),"One square meter of deck, cut into 12 equal parts"]),key:["24/5, which is 4 4/5. Write 8 as 8/1 and multiply across: 3 × 8 = 24 over 5. Or: 1/5 of 8 is 8/5, and three of those is 24/5. (5.NF.B.4)","6/12, which is the same as 1/2. Or: cancel the 3s first, which leaves 2/4 = 1/2. (5.NF.B.4)","35. 42 ÷ 6 = 7, and 7 × 5 = 35. Or: 5/6 × 42 = 210/6 = 35. (5.NF.B.4)","27 lanterns. 36 ÷ 4 = 9, and 9 × 3 = 27. Or: 3/4 × 36 = 108/4 = 27. (5.NF.B.4)","14/24, which is the same as 7/12. Or: cancel the 2 into the 8 first, which leaves 7/12. (5.NF.B.4)","3/4 × 2/3 = 6/12, which is 1/2 of a square meter. The square is cut into 4 columns and 3 rows, giving 12 equal parts, and the shaded patch covers 6 of them. Partial: the equation right with no area given is approaching. (5.NF.B.4, area model for a fraction times a fraction)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Getting the Stars Back",short:"Three Texts",parts:[{std:["RI.5.9"],n:3,dur:150,dir:"Read all three texts to yourself. Then answer out loud. Use facts from more than one text in each answer.",desc:"putting together information from several texts on the same topic",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">All three texts below are about the same topic: the light a town puts into the night sky.</p>${textBlock(S1,S2,S3)}`,qs:["Use all three texts. Explain why a town that wants its stars back does not have to turn its lights off.","Use at least two texts to explain how a shielded, warm-colored, timed streetlight helps an animal as well as an astronomer.","Which text would you use to find out what skyglow is. Which two would you use to argue that a town should change its lights? Explain your choices."],key:["Text 1: skyglow comes from light thrown sideways and upward that scatters off dust and water droplets, so it is stray light and not the lights themselves that is the problem. Text 2: a shielded cap sends the light down where it is wanted, warmer light scatters less, timers cut the late hours, and towns report the ground is just as bright. Text 3 gives a second reason the change is worth making, since the same stray light pulls night animals off course. Credit an answer that uses all three texts and names the shielding idea. (RI.5.9)","Text 2 describes the fix: light aimed down, warmer color, fewer lit hours. Text 3 names the harm that fix reduces: night-migrating birds pulled off course, moths worn out circling bulbs instead of pollinating, hatchlings crawling toward the brightest horizon. Text 1 explains why aiming light down cuts the glow an astronomer is fighting. (RI.5.9)","Text 1 to find out what skyglow is, because it is the only one that defines it. Texts 2 and 3 for the argument: Text 2 gives the practical fix and the evidence that it works, and Text 3 gives the harm to birds, insects, and sea turtles. (RI.5.9)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Pack the Crates",short:"Volume",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.5"],dur:150,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit with every answer.",desc:"volume of rectangular prisms, found by multiplying and by adding",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Folded lanterns are packed in wooden crates. A crate is a rectangular prism, and the lanterns inside sit in neat layers.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.prism(7,6,3),"A crate 7 cubes long, 6 cubes deep, and 3 cubes tall"]),qs:["A lantern box is 8 inches long, 5 inches wide, and 4 inches tall. What is its volume?","Look at the crate in the picture. It holds 3 layers of unit cubes, and each layer is 7 by 6. How many cubes fit in all? Write it two different ways.","A storage box has a volume of 72 cubic feet. Its base is 6 feet by 3 feet. How tall is the box?","Two boxes sit side by side. One is 4 by 3 by 2 units and the other is 5 by 3 by 2 units. What is their total volume?","A water tank is 10 centimeters on every edge. What is its volume?","Box A is 2 by 6 by 6 feet. Box B is 4 by 3 by 6 feet. Which box holds more?"],key:["160 cubic inches. 8 × 5 × 4 = 160. Or: 8 × 5 = 40 for the base, then 40 × 4. Partial: 160 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","126 cubes. One layer is 7 × 6 = 42, and 42 × 3 = 126. Written the other way, 7 × 6 × 3 = 126. Partial: one of the two ways written is approaching. (5.MD.C.5, volume as repeated layers and as a product of three edges)","4 feet. The base covers 6 × 3 = 18 square feet, and 72 ÷ 18 = 4. Or: 72 ÷ 6 = 12, then 12 ÷ 3 = 4. (5.MD.C.5)","54 cubic units. 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 and 5 × 3 × 2 = 30, then 24 + 30 = 54. Volume adds when solids do not overlap. Partial: one of the two boxes worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5, volume is additive)","1,000 cubic centimeters. 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000. Partial: 1,000 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","Neither. Both hold 72 cubic feet, because 2 × 6 × 6 = 72 and 4 × 3 × 6 = 72. Partial: naming neither with only one volume worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Dark Sky Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:170,dir:"Read both sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"notes in the student's own words from two sources, a list of sources, and a spoken summary",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Question: why does it matter how a town aims its outdoor lights?</p>${sourceBlock(S1,S3)}<ol class="qlist"><li>Write 3 short notes that answer the question. Use your own words. A note is a few words, not a whole sentence. Use both sources.</li><li>Under your notes, list your sources. Write the title and the author of each one.</li><li>Now look only at your notes. Answer the question out loud in one or two sentences.</li></ol>`,key:["Writes 3 notes that answer the question and draws on both sources, such as: stray light goes sideways and up; it scatters off dust and droplets; only the brightest stars still show; night-migrating birds pulled off course; moths worn out circling bulbs; hatchlings crawl toward the brightest horizon. (W.5.8, gathers relevant information)","Notes are short and in the student's own words, not sentences copied out of the sources. (W.5.8, paraphrases in notes)","Lists both sources by title and author: The Vanishing Sky by Idris Falk, and “Night Work” by Caleb Mwangi. (W.5.8, provides a list of sources)","Sums the answer up out loud from the notes, in the student's own words, with correct facts: light aimed sideways and upward washes out the stars and sends night animals the wrong way, so aiming it down matters. (W.5.8, summarizes in finished work)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Logbook Fix-Ups",short:"Logbook Fix",parts:[{std:["L.5.1"],dur:90,dir:"Each logbook line needs one fix. Say the whole sentence out loud the right way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (verb tense shifts, perfect tenses, paired conjunctions)",qs:["Last night the crew carried the lanterns down, ties them to the rope, and lights them at dusk.","Since October, the planning group has meet every Tuesday.","By the time we reached the bridge, the crew already hung half the lanterns.","Not only was the rope wet, and the knots were frozen.","Say this with the right form of the verb plan: “By spring, the group ____ three night walks.”"],key:["Last night the crew carried the lanterns down, tied them to the rope, and lit them at dusk. (L.5.1, correcting a shift in verb tense)","Since October, the planning group has met every Tuesday. (L.5.1, present perfect tense)","By the time we reached the bridge, the crew had already hung half the lanterns. (L.5.1, past perfect tense)","Not only was the rope wet, but the knots were also frozen. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions not only and but also)","By spring, the group will have planned three night walks. (L.5.1, future perfect tense)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Night Sky Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📋",hint:"What skyglow is and what helps"},std:["W.5.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write a page that teaches a reader what skyglow is and what a town can do about it. Use at least three facts from the fact box, in your own words. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: light and the night sky",SKY_BOX)}${checklist(["Start by naming your topic","Put facts that go together next to each other","Use linking words: also, another, for example, because, as a result","Use the topic words correctly: skyglow, shielded, scatter, fixture","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly, and a title or heading counts as formatting (W.5.2)","Groups related information in an order that makes sense, such as the problem first and the fixes after (W.5.2)","Develops the topic with at least three facts from the box, written in the student's own sentences (W.5.2)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as also, another, for example, because, as a result (W.5.2)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.5.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"Ten minutes of dark"},std:["W.5.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">On the night of the walk, the town turns every streetlight down for ten minutes so that people on the bridge can see the sky. You are standing out there when the lights go low. Write the story of those ten minutes. Write 7 to 9 sentences.</p>${pills(["Set the scene","Then","After a while","By the end"])}${checklist(["Set the scene and tell who is there","Tell the events in a clear order, with time words","Use dialogue or a description of what someone thinks and feels","Use concrete words and sensory detail so the reader can picture it","Give the story an ending"])}`,key:["Orients the reader: who is there, where, and what is about to happen (W.5.3)","Events unfold in a clear order, helped by transition words such as then, after a while, by the end (W.5.3)","Uses dialogue or description to show what a character thinks or feels (W.5.3)","Uses concrete words, sensory detail, and precise description of the sky and the bridge (W.5.3)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from the events (W.5.3)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Unlike Denominators",short:"Unlike",parts:[{std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],n:4,dur:60,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions and mixed numbers with unlike denominators, in bare problems and in word problems, and checking an answer with benchmarks",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Cord comes on rolls. The crew writes down what is used as a fraction of a roll.</p>`,qs:["2/3 + 1/4","3 1/2 - 5/6","The crew used 3/4 of a roll of cord on the west end and 2/5 of a roll on the east end. How much cord did they use in all?","A helper wrote 2/5 + 1/2 = 3/7. Use benchmark fractions to show that answer cannot be right, then give the correct one."],key:["11/12. In twelfths, 2/3 is 8/12 and 1/4 is 3/12, and 8/12 + 3/12 = 11/12. Or: twenty-fourths work too, 16/24 + 6/24 = 22/24 = 11/12. Partial: 3/7 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1)","2 2/3. As an improper fraction, 3 1/2 is 7/2, which is 21/6, and 5/6 taken away leaves 16/6, which is 8/3, or 2 2/3. Or: take 1/2 off 3 1/2 to get 3, then take the other 1/3 off to get 2 2/3. (5.NF.A.1, mixed numbers)","1 3/20 rolls. In twentieths, 3/4 is 15/20 and 2/5 is 8/20, and 15/20 + 8/20 = 23/20, which is 1 3/20. Partial: 23/20 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, a word problem on the same whole)","It cannot be right, because 1/2 alone is already more than 3/7, and adding 2/5 on top can only make it larger. The helper added the tops and the bottoms. The correct answer is 9/10, from 4/10 + 5/10. Partial: showing it is wrong with no correct answer given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, estimating with benchmarks to judge reasonableness)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Cubes in the Crate",short:"Cubic Units",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the crate. Answer out loud.",desc:"understanding volume as an attribute of a solid figure and what a cubic unit measures",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">This crate is packed with unit cubes: 3 cubes long, 2 cubes deep, and 2 cubes tall.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.prism(3,2,2),"The crate, packed with unit cubes"]),qs:["What is a unit cube, and how much volume does one of them have?","This crate holds 12 unit cubes. What does it mean to say its volume is 12 cubic units?","Why is volume measured in cubic units instead of square units?"],key:["A cube that is 1 unit on every edge. One unit cube has a volume of 1 cubic unit. Partial: defining the cube with no volume given is approaching. (5.MD.C.3a)","It means 12 unit cubes fill the crate with no gaps and no overlaps. The count of the cubes is the volume. Partial: naming the count with nothing said about gaps and overlaps is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)","Because volume measures the space inside a solid, and the space is filled with cubes. Square units cover a flat surface, so they measure area, not the room inside. Partial: naming the space with nothing said about square units measuring area is approaching. (5.MD.C.3, volume as an attribute of solid figures)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Map the Bridge",short:"Grid",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a coordinate system: the origin, the two axes, and what each number in an ordered pair means",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew maps the bridge on a grid. The origin, at the corner, is the west end of the bridge. One step right is one meter east, and one step up is one meter north.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.coordGrid({min:0,max:10,points:[[3,4,"A"],[7,2,"B"]],size:360}),"Two hooks on the grid"]),qs:["What are the coordinates of point A, and what does each of the two numbers tell you?","Point B is at (7, 2). Starting at the origin, describe exactly how to get there.","Why does (3, 4) name a different place from (4, 3)?"],key:["(3, 4). The first number, 3, is how far to travel from the origin along the horizontal axis. The second number, 4, is how far to travel along the vertical axis. Partial: the pair right with only one number explained is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","From the origin, travel 7 units along the horizontal axis, then 2 units up along the vertical axis. The origin is where the two axes cross, at 0 on each one. Partial: the route right with nothing said about where the origin sits is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Because the order is fixed by the convention: the first number always goes with the horizontal axis and the second with the vertical one. Swapping them names a different point. Partial: saying they are different with no reason from the order is approaching. (5.G.A.1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Sum It Up, Say It Out",short:"Summarize",parts:[{title:"Summarize It",std:["SL.5.2"],n:3,dur:50,read:TALLY_READ,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then look at the graph. Answer out loud.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud and information presented in a graph",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read a short note about how the lantern count is kept. Then look at the graph.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Lanterns found loose the next morning",cats:["3 years ago","2 years ago","Last year","This year"],vals:[5,2,7,3],max:8,step:2}),"From the crew logbook"]),qs:["Summarize what I read, in one or two sentences.","Summarize what the graph shows, in one sentence.","Name one detail from what I read that your summary left out. Why did a summary not need it?"],key:["A summary, not a retelling: the count is taken from the east tower where the whole rope is visible, the counter marks tens so the place is not lost, and two counters compare on separate nights and recount if the numbers disagree. Repeating the note sentence by sentence is not a summary. (SL.5.2, summarizing a text read aloud)","The number of lanterns found loose changes a lot from year to year, with the most last year at 7 and the fewest two years ago at 2. Any one-sentence statement of the overall pattern counts. Reading off all four numbers is not a summary. (SL.5.2, information presented quantitatively)","Any real detail that was dropped, such as the east tower being the only full view, or losing the place about half the time when counting one by one. The reason should be about size and importance: a summary keeps the main points and leaves out supporting detail. (SL.5.2)"]},{title:"Say Where You Stand",std:["SL.5.4"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:45,dir:"Talk for about a minute. Speak clearly and take your time.",desc:"presenting an opinion out loud, with ideas in a logical order and facts and details to support it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two people count the lanterns on separate evenings and compare. Should the town keep doing it that way, or is one counter enough? Present your opinion.</p>${checklist(["Say your opinion first","Put your reasons in an order that builds","Support each reason with a fact or a detail","Speak clearly and slow enough to follow"])}`,key:["Presents an opinion and sequences the ideas logically, so a listener can follow the argument from one reason to the next. (SL.5.4, sequencing ideas logically)","Supports the opinion with appropriate facts and relevant descriptive details, such as how easy it is to lose the place past three hundred, or how often loose lanterns actually turn up. (SL.5.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace, loud enough to hear, with finished sentences. (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Ten Minutes of Dark",short:"Opinion",parts:[{std:["W.5.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:50,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion piece with a stated point of view, ordered reasons, linking words, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">On the night of the walk the town turns the streetlights down for ten minutes. Some neighbors want to make it a full half hour so that more stars come out. Others want to stop doing it at all, because the path is harder to see. What should the town do? Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${checklist(["State your opinion in the first sentence","Give reasons in an order that supports your point of view","Back each reason with a fact or a detail","Use linking words: because, therefore, in addition, for instance","End with a concluding sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear point of view about the ten minutes (W.5.1)","Gives reasons in a logical order that supports the point of view (W.5.1)","Supports the reasons with facts and details, which may come from the skyglow sources in this game or from the student's own knowledge (W.5.1)","Links opinion and reasons with words and phrases such as because, therefore, in addition, for instance, and ends with a concluding statement (W.5.1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Where the Puddle Went",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A puddle outside the hall is 2 m across on Monday morning. By Wednesday it has gone, and the ground underneath it is dry. Nobody mopped it and it did not rain.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("drop",84)}${F.icon("sun",84)}${F.icon("wind",84)}</div>`,qs:["Where did the water go? Is it still water?","Use particles too small to see to explain how a puddle leaves on its own.","A balloon tied off on New Year morning has gone soft two weeks later with no hole in it. Explain that the same way."],key:["Into the air as water vapour. It is still water, just spread out as particles far too small to see. (5-PS1-1)","The particles at the surface are always moving, and the fastest ones escape into the air. Over two days enough escape that the whole puddle has spread out into the air. (5-PS1-1)","The gas particles inside are small enough to slip between the particles of the rubber itself, a few at a time, so gas leaks out through the material with no hole big enough to see. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four cords of the same thickness were found in a box with the labels fallen off.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cord</th><th>Mass per metre</th><th>Soaks up water?</th><th>Stretches then springs back?</th><th>Holds 20 kg without breaking?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>V</td><td>9 g</td><td>yes, a lot</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>W</td><td>11 g</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>X</td><td>13 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Y</td><td>58 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four cords are cotton string, elastic shock cord, nylon rope, and steel cable.</p>`,qs:["Which cord is the steel cable, and which measurement settles it?","Which cord is the cotton string? Name two results that point to it.","Cords W and X are close in mass and both hold 20 kg. Which is the shock cord, and which test tells them apart?"],key:["Y. Its mass per metre is more than four times any of the others for the same thickness, which is what a metal cable would do. (5-PS1-3)","V. It soaks up a lot of water and it is the only one that cannot hold 20 kg, both of which fit cotton string. (5-PS1-3)","W is the shock cord. The stretch test tells them apart: W stretches and springs back, X does not. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Sealed and Checked",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class started the year with three sealed bottles on a balance. Nothing was opened for a month.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed bottle</th><th>What was inside</th><th>Day 1</th><th>Day 30</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>water and a fizzing tablet, which fizzed hard and then stopped</td><td>548 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>water left in a sunny window, now with drops all over the inside</td><td>410 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>warm water with a scoop of drink powder, stirred until clear</td><td>462 g</td><td>462 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Bottle 1 is hard and tight because of the gas inside. What does it weigh on day 30?","Bottle 2 is covered inside with drops and the pool at the bottom is smaller. What does it weigh?","If a bottle like number 1 had been left open, the weight would have dropped. Explain why, and say what that means about the sealed test."],key:["548 g. A gas formed inside, but the gas is matter and it is still in the sealed bottle, so no weight was gained or lost. (5-PS1-2)","410 g. Water moved from the pool to the walls as vapour and back again, all inside the sealed bottle, so the total is the same. (5-PS1-2)","The gas would escape into the room, carrying matter out of the bottle, so the bottle would weigh less. That is why the test has to be sealed: it is the only way to show the matter went somewhere rather than ceasing to exist. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three mixings on the demonstration bench.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mixing</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>plaster powder and water</td><td>a thick paste that grew warm and then set hard as stone; adding more water would not soften it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>salt and water, stirred</td><td>the salt vanished and the water stayed clear; left in a dish, the water went and square salt crystals were left</td></tr>
     <tr><td>steel wool and vinegar, then left in air</td><td>the steel wool went dark, then orange brown and crumbly, and warmed slightly</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two mixings made a new substance? Give the evidence for each.","Which one was only a mixture? What is the strongest piece of evidence for that?","Both the plaster and the steel wool warmed up. Explain why warming alone is not proof, and say what else each one gave you."],key:["The plaster and water, because it set hard and cannot be returned to powder and water, and the steel wool, because it changed colour and texture permanently. (5-PS1-4)","The salt and water. Letting the water go left square salt crystals behind, so the salt was there all along, unchanged. Getting the starting material back is the strongest evidence. (5-PS1-4)","Warming happens in some mixtures too, for instance when certain substances dissolve. The plaster also set permanently and the steel wool also changed colour and texture permanently, and those changes cannot be undone, which is what makes the case. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:23,title:"A Bean With No Soil",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class sprouted beans on damp paper towels in a jar in the window. There was no soil of any kind. They weighed one bean dry at the start and the whole dried plant at the end.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",76)}${F.icon("herb",84)}${F.icon("drop",76)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Day 1</th><th>Day 24</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the bean, then the dried plant</td><td>0.5 g</td><td>2.9 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the paper towel, dried</td><td>4.0 g</td><td>4.0 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How much dry weight did the plant gain? What happened to the dry weight of the paper towel?","There was no soil and the towel lost nothing. Where did the 2.4 g of new plant material come from?","Another group's bean sprouted in a sealed dark cupboard and after day 10 it stopped gaining and began to shrink. What was missing, and why does that support the argument?"],key:["The plant gained 2.4 g. The paper towel's dry weight did not change at all. (5-LS1-1)","From air and water. Once the stored food in the bean ran out, the plant built new material from carbon dioxide in the air and water from the towel, using energy from sunlight. (5-LS1-1)","Light. Without it the plant could only live on the food stored in the bean, and once that was used up it had no way to build more material, so it shrank. That shows building new material needs light along with air and water. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the chain. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">One path energy takes from a field to a breakfast table.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("wheat",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("hen",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("egg",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">Grain grown in summer is fed to hens, and the hens lay eggs.</p>`,qs:["Trace the energy in a breakfast egg back to its source.","The hen is warm to the touch and clucks about all day. Where does the energy for that come from, and where does it end up?","Someone says eating the grain directly gives you more of the sun's energy than eating the egg. Is that right? Explain."],key:["From the hen, which got it from the grain, which the wheat plant made using energy from sunlight. (5-PS3-1)","From the food it eats, which traces back to sunlight. Most of it is used for moving, growing, and keeping the hen's body warm, and leaves the hen as warmth. Only part of it ends up stored in the egg. (5-PS3-1)","Yes, in terms of energy. Most of the energy in the grain is used up by the hen for its own living, so only a small part reaches the egg. Eating the grain skips that loss. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"The Pond Through a Year",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">A model of the park pond, followed for a year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>water, air, and pond mud</td><td>pond weed and tiny floating plants</td><td>they take in carbon dioxide, water, and minerals</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pond weed and floating plants</td><td>snails, insect larvae, tadpoles</td><td>grazing and filtering</td></tr>
     <tr><td>snails and larvae</td><td>fish and ducks</td><td>eaten</td></tr>
     <tr><td>everything that dies, plus waste</td><td>bacteria and fungi in the mud</td><td>broken down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bacteria and fungi</td><td>water, air, and mud</td><td>minerals and gases released</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("frog",70)}${F.icon("duck",70)}${F.icon("snail",70)}${F.icon("herb",70)}</div>`,qs:["Name the decomposers in this model and say where they work.","Autumn leaves blow into the pond from the trees around it. Trace where that material goes over the following year.","The pond is dredged and all the mud is taken away. Predict two effects on this model.","This model has five rows but only four kinds of thing in it: plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment. Which row connects the decomposers to the environment?"],key:["Bacteria and fungi, working in the mud at the bottom. (5-LS2-1)","The leaves sink, decomposers in the mud break them down, which releases minerals into the water and gases into the air, and pond plants take those minerals up and grow, feeding the animals. (5-LS2-1)","The decomposers and the stored minerals go with the mud, so the plants have far less to take up and grow poorly, and dead material no longer breaks down quickly, so it builds up. The whole cycle slows until mud and decomposers return. (5-LS2-1)","The last row, where bacteria and fungi release minerals and gases back into the water, air, and mud. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Which Way Is Down Now",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">A new calendar is being hung. A string with a weight on the end is held beside the wall to check it is straight, and a dropped pencil rolls under the table.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("newYearCal",80)}${F.icon("pencil",80)}${F.icon("clock",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which way does the dropped pencil go, and which way does the weighted string point?","Why does a weighted string give a better vertical line than guessing by eye?","A globe is on the desk. Point to three places on it and say which way down is at each one. What do the three directions have in common?"],key:["Both go straight down toward the floor. (5-PS2-1)","Earth pulls the weight straight down, so the string lines up with down exactly. An eye can be fooled by a sloping floor or a crooked window frame. (5-PS2-1)","At each place down points away from where you are and in toward the globe's centre, so the three directions all point at the centre of Earth even though they point different ways in the room. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">A warm spell in January melts the snowpack on the hills above town in three days. The ground below is still frozen hard, so almost none of the water soaks in. It runs off the hillside into the river, which rises over its banks and spreads mud across the water meadow. Along the river, alder roots hold the bank together and the bank does not collapse.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("thermometer",72)}${F.icon("wave",72)}${F.icon("tree",72)}${F.icon("mountain",72)}</div>`,qs:["Why did the meltwater run off instead of soaking in? Name the two spheres involved in that part.","The river spreads mud across the meadow. Which spheres moved material, and from where to where?","Alder roots hold the bank. Which sphere is doing the holding, and which sphere is being protected?"],key:["The ground was frozen, so the spaces in the soil were full of ice and nothing could soak in. That is the hydrosphere meeting a frozen geosphere, with the atmosphere driving both the melt and the freeze. (5-ESS2-1)","The hydrosphere carried material from the geosphere, soil and mud off the hillside and the riverbed, and laid it down on the meadow, which affects the biosphere growing there. (5-ESS2-1)","The biosphere, the living roots, is holding the geosphere, the soil of the bank, in place against the hydrosphere. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Sky in January",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three stars, with the light each gives off and its distance.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance from Earth</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Sirius</td><td>about 25 units</td><td>8.6 light-years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Deneb</td><td>roughly 100,000 units</td><td>roughly 2,600 light-years</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">One light-year is about 63,000 times the distance from Earth to the sun.</p>`,qs:["Rank the three by the light they give off. Now rank them by how bright they look from Earth.","Deneb gives off roughly 100,000 times as much light as the sun but is only a modest star in the sky. Explain using the last line of the chart.","Two children look at Sirius and Deneb and agree Sirius is brighter. One concludes Sirius is the more powerful star. What is wrong with that reasoning?"],key:["By light given off: Deneb, then Sirius, then the sun. By how bright they look: the sun by an enormous margin, then Sirius, then Deneb. The two rankings are almost reversed. (5-ESS1-1)","A light-year is about 63,000 times the Earth to sun distance, so 2,600 light-years is unimaginably further than 8 light-minutes. Light spreads out as it travels, and that difference in distance swamps the 100,000 times advantage in light given off. (5-ESS1-1)","Looking brighter only tells you how much light reaches your eye, which depends on distance as well as on the star. Deneb is hundreds of times further away, and it actually gives off thousands of times more light than Sirius. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">Daylight was recorded on the first of each month at the start of the new year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Sunrise</th><th>Sunset</th><th>Hours of daylight</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1 January</td><td>7:04</td><td>16:55</td><td>9.9</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1 February</td><td>6:49</td><td>17:25</td><td>10.6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1 March</td><td>6:17</td><td>17:53</td><td>11.6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1 April</td><td>6:34</td><td>19:21</td><td>12.8</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in the hours of daylight over these four months.","Sunrise got earlier from January to March, then later again on 1 April, yet daylight still went up. What explains that?","The noon shadow of the flagpole shortened every month across this chart. Explain why the shadow and the daylight go together."],key:["Daylight grows every month, from 9.9 hours to 12.8, gaining roughly an hour a month and speeding up slightly. (5-ESS1-2)","The clocks moved forward an hour for daylight saving between 1 March and 1 April, which pushed both sunrise and sunset later on the clock. Sunset moved later by more than sunrise did, so the total daylight still rose. Accept any answer that notices sunset gained more than sunrise lost. (5-ESS1-2)","Both depend on how high the midday sun climbs. As the year moves on from the shortest day, the sun rises higher at noon, which both shortens the noon shadow and keeps the sun above the horizon longer. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:27,title:"A Jug of All the Water",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">Imagine a 1,000 millilitre jug holding all the water on Earth.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Reservoir</th><th>Out of 1,000 ml</th></tr>
     <tr><td>salty ocean</td><td>970 ml</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ice caps and glaciers</td><td>21 ml</td></tr>
     <tr><td>groundwater</td><td>9 ml</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lakes, rivers, soil, and air</td><td>less than 1 ml, about 20 drops</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How many millilitres of the jug are fresh water? Turn that into a percentage.","All the lakes and rivers in the world are about 20 drops out of this jug. Why is that tiny amount so important?","Imagine showing this model as a graph instead of a jug. What kind of graph would make the point clearly? What would be hard about it?"],key:["About 30 ml of the 1,000 ml, which is about 3%. That is the 21 ml of ice plus the 9 ml of groundwater plus the drops. (5-ESS2-2)","Because it is the water people, land animals, and land plants actually reach and use, and it is refilled constantly as rain and snowmelt flow through. A small store with water moving through it fast still supplies everything. (5-ESS2-2)","A bar graph makes the ocean's size obvious at a glance. The hard part is that the other three bars are so small they are almost invisible, so a second graph of just the fresh water, or a broken scale, is needed to show them. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two towns made a change at the start of the year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Town</th><th>What they did</th><th>Result after one year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Northgate</td><td>replaced every street lamp with a shielded lamp that points its light down</td><td>electricity for lighting down by half, the same brightness on the pavement, and far more stars visible from town</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Southgate</td><td>added a bus every ten minutes on the main road and a protected bike lane beside it</td><td>cars entering the centre down by a fifth, and air quality readings improved</td></tr></table>`,qs:["A shielded lamp points its light down. Explain how that can use half the electricity and still light the pavement as well.","Southgate did not ban anything. Why would adding buses and a bike lane still cut the number of cars?","Combine the two reports into a plan for a third town that wants to cut both its electricity use and its traffic."],key:["The old lamps threw much of their light sideways and up into the sky, where it did nothing useful. Pointing all of it down means a smaller lamp delivers the same light to the pavement. (5-ESS3-1)","People choose whatever is quickest and easiest. A bus every ten minutes and a safe bike lane make those options good enough that a fifth of drivers switch without being forced. (5-ESS3-1)","Fit shielded downward lamps to cut lighting electricity, and add frequent buses with a protected bike lane to cut car traffic and improve air quality. The plan has to draw on each report. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Design a Boot Rack",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"Thirty pairs of wet boots are left in the hall entry every day. By the next morning they are still wet and cold, the floor is soaked, and the whole entry smells. The caretaker wants something that holds thirty pairs off the floor and dries them overnight. It has to fit in a space two metres wide and one metre deep, it must cost no more than the money already in the maintenance tin, and it has to be safe to leave running with nobody in the building.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name three things the finished rack must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the caretaker has to work inside."],key:["Thirty pairs of wet boots are left in the entry overnight and are still wet in the morning, and the water ends up on the floor, so the entry needs a way to hold and dry them. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any three criteria from the story: holds thirty pairs off the floor, dries them overnight, and is safe to leave running unattended. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: it must fit a space two metres wide and one metre deep, and it must cost no more than the money in the maintenance tin. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, each tested with thirty pairs of equally wet boots overnight.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Pairs it holds</th><th>Dry by morning</th><th>Footprint</th><th>Cost</th><th>Safe left running?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: open timber shelves with gaps</td><td>30</td><td>9 of 30</td><td>1.8 m by 0.9 m</td><td>cheap</td><td>nothing to run</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: timber shelves plus one small low-heat fan heater on a timer</td><td>30</td><td>28 of 30</td><td>1.8 m by 0.9 m</td><td>within the tin</td><td>yes, low heat on a timer</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: a bought heated drying cabinet</td><td>36</td><td>36 of 36</td><td>2.2 m by 1.1 m</td><td>far over the tin</td><td>yes</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design dries best? Name two reasons it is still not the choice here.","Design A is cheapest and safest. Name the criterion it fails, and say why that criterion is the point of the whole project.","Which design would you recommend, and list the criteria and limits it satisfies."],key:["C dries every pair, but it costs far more than the money in the tin and it is bigger than the space available, so it breaks both limits. (3-5-ETS1-2)","It only dries 9 pairs of 30. Drying the boots overnight was the main thing the rack had to do, so a rack that only stores them has not solved the problem. (3-5-ETS1-2)","B. It holds 30 pairs off the floor, dries 28 of 30 overnight, fits the 2 m by 1 m space, costs no more than the tin, and is safe to leave on a timer at low heat. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The caretaker tested where to aim the airflow.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Night 1</th><th>Night 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>airflow aimed</td><td>across the front of the shelves</td><td>up through the shelves from below</td></tr>
     <tr><td>boots on the rack</td><td>30 pairs, soaked for the same 10 minutes</td><td>30 pairs, soaked for the same 10 minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>hours running</td><td>8</td><td>8</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pairs dry by morning</td><td>14</td><td>28</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>the back row stayed soaking</td><td>the two pairs on the end shelf stayed damp</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What was held the same, and what was changed? Why does that make the test fair?","Suppose night 2 had run for twelve hours instead of eight. Could you still say the airflow direction made the difference? Explain.","Name a change that would fix the failure on each night."],key:["The number of boots, how long they were soaked, and the hours running were all held the same, and only the direction of the airflow changed, so the difference in pairs dried can be traced to the direction. (3-5-ETS1-3)","No. Two things would have changed, direction and running time, so you could not tell which one dried more boots. That is not a fair test. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Night 1 left the back row wet, so aim the air from below or add a second outlet reaching the back. Night 2 left the end shelf damp, so move the heater toward the middle, add a duct along the rack, or leave the end shelf for drier footwear. Any fix tied to the stated failure counts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"How We Know About Long Ago",short:"How We Know",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing pre-Columbian settlements and the kinds of evidence we know about them from",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nobody in North America was writing down history in books before 1492. So how do we know about the nations who lived here?</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Kind of evidence</th><th>What it can tell us</th><th>What it cannot</th></tr>
     <tr><td>standing buildings, such as the cliff dwellings at Mesa Verde</td><td>how people built, how many lived together, how rooms were used</td><td>what people said or believed in them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>objects left in the ground: tools, pots, seeds, bones</td><td>what people ate, made, and traded, and roughly when</td><td>names, languages, or decisions</td></tr>
     <tr><td>oral history passed down within a nation</td><td>names, events, places, relationships, and meaning</td><td>exact dates in the way a written record gives them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>accounts written by Europeans after 1492</td><td>what one visitor saw and understood</td><td>a full or neutral picture, since the writer was an outsider with his own aims</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why can an archaeologist learn what people ate without any writing at all?","Oral history is listed as evidence. Name one thing it gives us that objects in the ground cannot.","Why does a historian treat a European account of a nation differently from an account by someone of that nation?","Pick two kinds of evidence and explain how using both together gives a better picture than either alone."],key:["Seeds, animal bones, cooking pots, and the wear on tools survive in the ground, and each one is direct physical evidence of what was grown, hunted, and cooked. Partial: naming one kind of remain with nothing said about it being direct evidence is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Names, relationships, events, and what things meant to the people themselves. A pot cannot tell you who made it or why it mattered; a story carried down within a nation can. Partial: saying it tells stories with no account of what objects cannot carry is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","The European writer was an outsider who did not speak the language, stayed briefly, and usually had aims of his own, such as claiming land or justifying an expedition. His account is still evidence, but it needs weighing against what the nation itself says. Partial: calling the account biased with no reason from the writer's position is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Any supported pair. For example buildings plus oral history gives both the physical village and the names and meaning attached to it, and objects plus a written account lets a historian check one against the other. Partial: two kinds named with no account of what using both adds is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Who Claimed What",short:"Claims",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing early explorations to the regions each European power claimed and why",html:()=>`<p class="big">By 1700 four European powers claimed parts of North America, and each claim traced back to where its explorers had gone.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Power</th><th>Claimed</th><th>Traced back to</th><th>Main interest</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Spain</td><td>Florida, Mexico, and the Southwest</td><td>Columbus, Ponce de Leon, Coronado, de Soto</td><td>gold, silver, and land</td></tr>
     <tr><td>France</td><td>the Saint Lawrence, the Great Lakes, and down the Mississippi</td><td>Cartier, Champlain, La Salle</td><td>furs</td></tr>
     <tr><td>England</td><td>the Atlantic coast from Maine to Georgia</td><td>Cabot, and later settlement companies</td><td>farmland and settlement</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the Netherlands</td><td>the Hudson valley, until England took it in 1664</td><td>Hudson</td><td>furs and trade</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("map",76)}${F.icon("flag",76)}${F.icon("globe",76)}</div>`,qs:["Match each power to the river or coast its claim was built around.","France claimed an enormous area but sent far fewer settlers than England. How does the interest column explain that?","Explain what it means for a European country to claim land, when nations were already living there.","The Dutch claim disappeared in 1664. What does that tell you about how secure these claims were?"],key:["Spain around Florida and the Southwest, France along the Saint Lawrence, the Great Lakes, and the Mississippi, England along the Atlantic coast, and the Netherlands in the Hudson valley. Partial: two or three of the four powers matched is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Furs need trading posts and good relations with the nations who do the trapping, not farms. England wanted farmland, which needs settlers who clear and occupy ground, so England's interest drew people and France's did not. Partial: one of the two interests explained is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","It meant the European country told other European countries that this area was theirs to settle and trade in. The nations already living there had not agreed to it and in most cases did not know of it, so the claim was a statement between European powers rather than anything the people on the land accepted. Partial: saying they said it was theirs with nothing said about the nations already there is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Not secure at all. A claim held only as long as the country could defend it, so land changed hands by war and treaty between European powers, again without the agreement of the people living there. Partial: saying it changed hands with nothing said about defending the claim is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing two ways of recording an agreement and explaining the conflict that followed",html:()=>`<p class="big">American Indian nations and colonists both recorded agreements, but in different ways.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Wampum belt</th><th>Written deed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>what it is</td><td>a belt of shell beads worked into a pattern that records the agreement</td><td>a paper in English describing boundaries, signed and witnessed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>how it is read</td><td>a keeper who knows the pattern recites the agreement at gatherings so it stays known</td><td>read by anyone who reads English and has the paper</td></tr>
     <tr><td>how it is kept</td><td>held by the nation and renewed in person, often with gifts and speeches</td><td>filed away, and produced later as proof</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what counts as the agreement</td><td>the whole relationship, renewed over time</td><td>the exact words on the page</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name one strength of each way of recording an agreement.","A wampum belt treats an agreement as a relationship to be renewed. A deed treats it as fixed words. Explain how that difference could cause a dispute years later.","Only one side could read English. Why did that matter so much?","Both sides thought they were keeping the agreement. Use the chart to explain how that was possible."],key:["A belt keeps the agreement alive in people's memory through regular recitation, so the whole community knows it. A deed records exact boundaries in a form anyone who reads the language can check later. Partial: one of the two strengths named is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","A nation might expect the agreement to be revisited and adjusted as circumstances changed, while the colonists held that the words signed once stood for ever. Each would then accuse the other of breaking it. Partial: naming the difference with no dispute traced from it is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","The side that wrote and read the document controlled what it said and what it was later claimed to say, and an interpreter stood between the two. A nation could agree to one thing and find the paper said another. Partial: saying it was unfair with no account of who controlled the wording is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Each was keeping the agreement as its own system understood it. One was renewing a relationship; the other was holding to fixed words. The record itself, not bad faith alone, could put them at odds. Partial: blaming one side for bad faith is not the answer the question asks for; naming the two systems with no link to the conflict is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Trade, Debt and a War",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:150,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the economic institutions that linked the colonies to Britain and the Atlantic",html:()=>`<p class="big">Britain's rules were built on an idea called mercantilism: colonies should supply raw materials and buy finished goods from the home country.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Direction</th><th>What moved</th></tr>
     <tr><td>colonies to Britain</td><td>tobacco, rice, indigo, timber, furs, fish, iron ore</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Britain to colonies</td><td>cloth, tools, glass, paper, finished metal goods, tea</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rules</td><td>certain goods could only be shipped to Britain, in British or colonial ships</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the forced trade across the Atlantic</td><td>enslaved Africans carried to the Americas against their will, with goods from the Americas sold on in Europe</td></tr>
     <tr><td>who the traders were</td><td>Quaker merchant families in Philadelphia dealt with Quakers in London and the West Indies, lending each other money and goods on the strength of belonging to the same religious society; in the colonies with an established church, a parish tax was collected from everyone to pay the local minister</td></tr>
   </table>`,qs:["Describe mercantilism in your own words, using the first two rows.","A Philadelphia Quaker merchant shipped goods to a Quaker in London on credit. According to the chart, what did belonging to the same religious society give the two of them that two strangers did not have? And why would a rule that certain goods could only be shipped to Britain annoy that merchant?","Raw materials are usually worth less than finished goods. Explain what that meant for who profited most from this system.","The last row describes the forced trade in enslaved people. Explain how it fitted into the rest of the system."],key:["The colonies send out raw materials they grow, cut, or dig, and buy back manufactured goods made in Britain, with rules in place to keep the trade inside the empire. Partial: one direction of the trade described is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Belonging to the same religious society gave them a reason to trust each other across an ocean, so they would lend money and goods on credit without being able to check up on each other. On the rule: he could not sell to whoever paid most, so he lost better prices in other countries and had to accept whatever British buyers offered. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the trust half with nothing on the shipping rule sits there. (HSS-5.4)","The side that turns raw material into finished goods captures most of the value, so British manufacturers and merchants profited most, while colonial growers and shippers took the smaller share. Partial: naming who profited with no account of where the value is added is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Plantations growing tobacco, rice, sugar, and indigo for the Atlantic trade depended on forced labour, and ships carried goods and enslaved people between Africa, the Americas, and Europe on connected voyages. The forced trade in people was not separate from the economy; it was built into it. Partial: describing the trade with nothing said about how it fitted the rest of the system is approaching. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the economic causes of the American Revolution",html:()=>`<p class="big">Britain came out of the 1754 to 1763 war against France victorious and deeply in debt.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Figure or detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>British national debt in 1763</td><td>roughly double what it had been in 1754</td></tr>
     <tr><td>cost of keeping soldiers in North America after 1763</td><td>a large yearly sum, paid by British taxpayers</td></tr>
     <tr><td>taxes paid by people in Britain</td><td>far higher per person than taxes paid in the colonies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Parliament's conclusion</td><td>the colonies had benefited from the war and should pay a share</td></tr></table>`,qs:["State Parliament's argument for taxing the colonies, using the chart.","State the colonists' answer to that argument.","People in Britain paid higher taxes than colonists did. Why did colonists still see the new taxes as unfair?","Was this argument really about the amount of money? Give a reason for your answer."],key:["Britain had doubled its debt winning a war fought partly in North America and was still paying to keep soldiers there, while British taxpayers paid far more per person than colonists did, so the colonies should contribute. Partial: naming the debt with nothing said about the soldiers or the comparison of taxes is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","That they had no elected members in Parliament, so Parliament had no right to tax them. Their own assemblies should decide colonial taxes, as they always had. Partial: saying they did not want to pay with no account of representation is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Because the objection was about who had the right to decide, not the size of the bill. A tax from a body they had not elected broke what they saw as a basic right, whatever the amount. Partial: arguing about the amount is the misreading to watch for; it is approaching, not proficient. (HSS-5.5)","Mostly no, and a student may argue either way with a reason. The sums were modest and some taxes were repealed, yet the dispute carried on, because the real question was whether Parliament could tax them at all. Partial: a verdict with no reason is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing the strengths and weaknesses of each side in the Revolution",html:()=>`<p class="big">On paper the two sides were badly matched. The result was not as one-sided as the list suggests.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Britain</th><th>The thirteen states</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the strongest navy in the world and a large trained army</td><td>a small army, often short of pay, food, and shoes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>money, credit, and hired soldiers from the German states</td><td>a Congress that could ask the states for money but not require it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>supplies had to cross 3,000 miles of ocean</td><td>fighting on home ground, with local knowledge and local supplies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>needed to defeat the rebellion completely</td><td>only needed to avoid losing until Britain gave up</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name Britain's two greatest advantages and the states' two greatest advantages.","The last row is the most important one for understanding the result. Explain why.","Explain how a 3,000 mile supply line could turn an advantage into a problem.","Which single factor do you think mattered most to the outcome? Give a reason from the chart."],key:["Britain: the strongest navy and a large trained army, plus money and credit. The states: fighting on home ground with local supplies and knowledge, and needing only to survive rather than to win outright. Partial: one side's two advantages named is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Because the two sides needed different things. Britain had to crush the rebellion everywhere; the states only had to keep an army in the field until the cost and the politics made Britain stop. A draw was a win for one side and a loss for the other. Partial: naming the row with no account of the two different aims is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Every soldier, musket, and barrel of food had to be shipped across the Atlantic, which took weeks, cost enormous sums, and could be intercepted. A big army far from home is expensive and slow to reinforce. Partial: saying it was far away with nothing said about cost, time or interception is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any supported answer: the asymmetry in what each side needed to achieve, the French alliance and its fleet, the distance of Britain's supply line, or the home ground advantage. The reason from the chart is what counts. Partial: a factor named with no reason from the chart is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:32,title:"The Preamble and the Gold Rush",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the words. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining what the Preamble to the Constitution says the government is for",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Constitution opens with one long sentence, the Preamble. It sets out six purposes.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Phrase</th><th>In plain words</th></tr>
     <tr><td>form a more perfect Union</td><td>bind the states together better than the Articles had</td></tr>
     <tr><td>establish Justice</td><td>set up courts and laws that treat people fairly</td></tr>
     <tr><td>insure domestic Tranquility</td><td>keep peace at home</td></tr>
     <tr><td>provide for the common defence</td><td>defend the country as a whole</td></tr>
     <tr><td>promote the general Welfare</td><td>work for the good of everyone, not one group</td></tr>
     <tr><td>secure the Blessings of Liberty to ourselves and our Posterity</td><td>protect freedom for us and for the people who come after us</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The sentence begins We the People of the United States.</p>`,qs:["The Preamble begins We the People rather than We the States. Why would that choice of words matter?","Pick two of the six purposes and give a present-day example of government doing that job.","The last purpose mentions posterity, meaning people not yet born. What does including them change about how the writers were thinking?","In 1787 We the People did not include most of the people living in the country. Say who was left out, and what happened to that phrase later."],key:["It says the government's authority comes from the people directly, not from an agreement among state governments, which made it a national government rather than a league of states. Partial: noting the wording with nothing said about where the authority comes from is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Any two supported examples: courts and a jury system for justice, armed forces or coastguard for the common defence, police and emergency services for peace at home, roads, public health, or schools for the general welfare. Partial: one purpose matched to a present-day example is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","It means they were writing rules meant to outlast them, not just to settle their own argument, which is why they included an amendment process and made the document hard to change on a whim. Partial: defining posterity with nothing said about what it changes is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Women, enslaved people, most free Black people, American Indian people, and men without property were all excluded from voting and from the convention. Over the following centuries amendments and long campaigns widened who the phrase actually covered, and arguments about it continue. Partial: naming who was left out with nothing said about what happened later is approaching. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how the Gold Rush changed California's population and how it became a state",html:()=>`<p class="big">Gold was found at a sawmill on the American River in January 1848. News reached the east coast that autumn.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1848</td><td>gold found; a few thousand people arrive from nearby</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1849</td><td>roughly 80,000 people arrive from the east, from Mexico, from Chile, from China, and from Europe</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>California is admitted as a state, skipping the usual territory stage</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850s</td><td>San Francisco grows from a village into a city; mining taxes and laws target Chinese and Mexican miners; American Indian people in California lose land and population at a devastating rate</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two different routes or starting points people used to get to California in 1849.","California skipped the usual territory stage and became a state in 1850. What in the chart explains why that was possible?","A gold rush is an economic incentive. Explain what that phrase means using this example.","The last row names people who lost rather than gained. Say who they were and what happened to them."],key:["Any two: by ship around South America, by ship to Panama and across the isthmus, overland by wagon trail, or by sea from China, Chile, or Mexico. Partial: one route named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","The population jumped by tens of thousands in a single year, which put it well past the 60,000 people a territory needed before it could apply for statehood. Partial: naming the rise with no link to the 60,000 threshold is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","An economic incentive is a chance to gain money or property that makes people act. The possibility of finding gold was strong enough that tens of thousands sold what they had and travelled thousands of miles on the chance. Partial: defining the phrase with no use of this example is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Chinese and Mexican miners were taxed and restricted by laws aimed at them, and American Indian people in California were driven off their land and died in enormous numbers from violence, disease, and the destruction of the places they lived on. Partial: one of the two groups described is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating Pacific and noncontiguous states and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",96)}${F.icon("map",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">Questions about the Pacific side of the country.</p>`,qs:["Name the five states that touch the Pacific Ocean.","What is the capital of California, and what is the capital of Washington?","What is the capital of Hawaii, and what is the capital of Alaska?","Which state is the largest by area, and which state is the largest by population?","How many states are there in all, and which two were the last to join?"],key:["California, Oregon, Washington, Alaska, and Hawaii. Partial: three or four of the five named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Sacramento, California and Olympia, Washington. Note if the student names Los Angeles or Seattle, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Honolulu, Hawaii and Juneau, Alaska. Note if the student names Anchorage, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Alaska is the largest by area. California is the largest by population. Partial: one of the two answered is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","50 states. Alaska and Hawaii were the last two to join, both in 1959. Partial: the count right with one of the two states named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=FLUENCY.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[0].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Cite the Evidence",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the story and cite your evidence.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>What kind of sister is Amara? Cite two details from the story that support your answer.</li><li>The story never says that the list still matters to Nilay. What evidence in the story supports that idea?</li></ol>${F.passage(LIST,LIST_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Careful with him, and observant. Any two details: she did not laugh when she found the list, she read the four lines twice before saying anything, she marked only one line and told him it was already done, and she said “You just have not noticed,” which shows she had been watching. Two cited details are needed. (RL.6.1, cites evidence to support an analysis)","He kept it folded in the back cover rather than throwing it away, he wrote his own list after throwing the school's card away, and at the end “He looked at the check for a long time, and he did not erase it.” (RL.6.1, inference with cited evidence)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Evidence in the Article",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite evidence from the article.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The article never uses the word telescope. Cite two details that show a light year is a working tool for astronomers, not just a fun fact.</li><li>Does the article claim that a light year is easier to use than miles only because the number is shorter? Cite the evidence for your answer.</li></ol>${F.passage(LY,LY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Any two: astronomers use it because the numbers in space get unwieldy fast, writing a star distance in miles takes a string of digits nobody can hold in their head, and the unit carries the travel time of the light for free. Two cited details are needed. (RI.6.1, cites evidence to support an analysis)","No. The article says the unit “carries a second piece of information for free,” because a distance in light years is also how long the light has been traveling. The shorter number is only part of the reason. (RI.6.1, cites evidence)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Divide the Fractions",std:["6.NS.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.B.3"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>3/4 ÷ 1/8 = ?</li><li>How many lanterns that each hold 2/3 of a liter can be filled from 6 liters of oil?</li><li>4 people share 2/5 of a kilogram of wax equally. How much does each person get?</li></ol>`,key:["6. 3/4 ÷ 1/8 = 3/4 × 8/1 = 24/4 = 6. (6.NS.A.1)","9 lanterns. 6 ÷ 2/3 = 6 × 3/2 = 18/2 = 9. (6.NS.A.1)","1/10 of a kilogram. 2/5 ÷ 4 = 2/5 × 1/4 = 2/20 = 1/10. (6.NS.A.1)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Fractional Edges",std:["6.G.A.2"],needs:["5.MD.C.5"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A folded lantern box is 3/4 foot long, 1/2 foot wide, and 2/3 foot tall. What is its volume?</li><li>A box is 1 1/2 inches by 2 inches by 3 inches. What is its volume?</li></ol>`,key:["1/4 of a cubic foot. 3/4 × 1/2 × 2/3 = 6/24 = 1/4. (6.G.A.2, volume with fractional edge lengths)","9 cubic inches. 3/2 × 2 × 3 = 18/2 = 9. (6.G.A.2)"]},14:{id:"L14",title:"Level Up: Pronoun Patrol",std:["L.6.1"],needs:["L.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the right way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix it: “If a counter loses the tally, you should start over at the west end.”</li><li>Fix it: “Her and me walked the rope twice.”</li><li>Add one word to show that nobody helped: “We found the three loose lanterns ____.”</li></ol>`,key:["If a counter loses the tally, he or she should start over at the west end. Also correct: If counters lose the tally, they should start over at the west end. (L.6.1, fixing a shift in pronoun person)","She and I walked the rope twice. (L.6.1, pronouns in the subjective case)","We found the three loose lanterns ourselves. (L.6.1, intensive pronoun)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","W.5.8","W.5.2","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0],pvVal="";function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const PV_COLS=["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousandths"];function pvTable(){const raw=pvVal.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,""),dot=raw.indexOf(".");const whole=(dot<0?raw:raw.slice(0,dot)).slice(-4),frac=(dot<0?"":raw.slice(dot+1)).slice(0,3);let h="<tr>";PV_COLS.forEach(c=>h+=`<th>${c}</th>`);h+="</tr><tr>";for(let i=0;i<4;i++){const k=whole.length-(4-i);h+=`<td>${k>=0?whole[k]:""}</td>`}for(let i=0;i<3;i++)h+=`<td>${i<frac.length?frac[i]:""}</td>`;return h+"</tr>"}function pvHtml(){return`<label class="pv-in">Type a number<input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="pvInput" value="${pvVal}" autocomplete="off"></label><div class="tool-scroll"><table class="pv" id="pvTable">${pvTable()}</table></div><p class="small">Each place to the left is worth 10 times as much. Each place to the right is worth one tenth as much.</p>`}function strips(){const dens=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,12],W=600,h=34;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+8} ${dens.length*(h+6)+4}" width="100%" style="max-width:640px" role="img" aria-label="Fraction strips for halves, thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths, eighths, and twelfths">`;dens.forEach((d,r)=>{for(let k=0;k<d;k++){const x=4+k*W/d,y=2+r*(h+6);s+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${W/d}" height="${h}" fill="${r%2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x+W/d/2}" y="${y+h/2}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="${d>8?14:17}" fill="var(--ink)">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</text>`}});return s+"</svg>"}const frames=list=>`<ul class="starters">${list.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function sentHtml(st){const p=st&&st.parts&&st.parts[0],k=p&&p.alt&&p.alt.key||p&&p.std&&p.std[0]||"RL.5.1";if(k==="facts"||k==="W.5.2")return frames(["Skyglow is ____.","It happens because ____.","One thing a town can do is ____.","For example, ____.","As a result, ____."]);if(k==="story"||k==="W.5.3")return frames(["When the lights went low, ____.","At first, ____.","Then ____.","“____,” said ____.","By the end of the ten minutes, ____."]);if(k==="W.5.8")return frames(["Note: ____ (Source ____)","My sources: 1. ____ by ____. 2. ____ by ____.","It matters how a town aims its lights because ____ and ____."]);if(k==="RI.5.9")return frames(["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","Text 3 says ____.","Put together, ____."]);if(k==="RI.5.1"||k==="RI.6.1")return frames(["The article says, “____.”","I know ____ because the article says, “____.”"]);if(k==="W.5.1")return frames(["The town should ____.","One reason is ____.","For instance, ____.","In addition, ____.","Therefore ____."]);if(k==="SL.5.2")return frames(["The main point was ____.","The graph shows ____.","In short, ____."]);if(k==="SL.5.4")return frames(["I think the town should ____.","My first reason is ____.","A fact that backs that up is ____.","That is why ____."]);return frames(["The story says, “____.”","I know ____ because the story says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."])}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Prefix, suffix, and root chart",std:["RF.5.3"],html:()=>F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["un-, im-, non-","not or the opposite of"],["re-","again or back"],["pre-","before"],["mis-","wrongly"],["trans-","across"],["dis-","apart or not"],["-tion, -ion","the act or result of"],["-able, -ible","can be"],["-ous","full of"],["lumin","light"],["astro","star"],["tele, scope","far, see"],["dict","say"],["flect","bend"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the prefix and the suffix, read the middle, then put the word back together.</p>`},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["5.OA.A.1","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart to thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],html:pvHtml,bind:b=>{const inp=b.querySelector("#pvInput"),tb=b.querySelector("#pvTable");if(inp&&tb)inp.oninput=()=>{pvVal=inp.value.replace(/[^0-9.]/g,"");tb.innerHTML=pvTable()}}},strips:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:strips},fdiv:{name:"Fraction formula card",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>A fraction is a division problem: a/b = a ÷ b.</li><li>Improper to mixed: 29/8 means 29 ÷ 8, which is 3 with 5 left over, so 3 5/8.</li><li>Fraction times fraction: multiply the tops, then multiply the bottoms.</li><li>A whole number can be written over 1: 8 = 8/1.</li><li>“Of” means multiply: 3/4 of 36 is 3/4 × 36.</li></ul>`},order:{name:"Order of operations card",std:["5.OA.A.1"],html:()=>`<ol class="starters"><li>Work the innermost grouping symbol first: parentheses ( ), then brackets [ ], then braces { }.</li><li>Inside a grouping, multiply and divide before you add and subtract.</li><li>Rewrite the whole expression after each step so nothing gets lost.</li><li>Check: did the grouping symbols change the order? If not, say so.</li></ol>`},vol:{name:"Volume card",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],html:()=>F.table(["What to find","How"],[["Volume of a box","length × width × height, answer in cubic units"],["Volume by layers","area of the base × the number of layers"],["A missing height","volume ÷ the area of the base"],["Two solids together","find each volume, then add them"]])+`<p class="small">Volume counts the unit cubes that fit inside. The answer is always in cubic units.</p>`},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3","W.5.8","RI.5.9","RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1","SL.5.2","SL.5.4"],html:sentHtml},figs:{name:"Simile and metaphor card",std:["RL.5.4"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>Simile: compares two things with like or as. “The rope swung like a jump rope.”</li><li>Metaphor: says one thing is another thing. “The river was a second sky.”</li><li>Ask: what two things are compared? How are they alike? What does the comparison make you feel?</li></ul>`},tense:{name:"Verb tense card",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],html:()=>F.table(["Tense","How to build it","Example"],[["Past perfect","had + verb","The crew had already left."],["Present perfect","has or have + verb","The group has met every week."],["Future perfect","will have + verb","By spring we will have finished."],["Tense shift","keep one time through the sentence","We carried and tied, not carried and ties."],["Paired words","not only / but also, both / and, either / or, neither / nor","Not only was it cold, but it was also wet."]])}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.5.3"],msg:"The chart gives the meaning of the prefixes, suffixes, and roots, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["5.OA.A.1","5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.MD.C.5","6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products. Rate on whether the student sets the problem up correctly and can explain it."},{t:"pv",std:["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The place value chart names the places and lines the digits up, so the student does not have to recall place names. The ten times and one tenth reasoning, and the comparing, still need the student's own thinking."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"With fraction strips the student can see the size of each share. Rate on whether the student can write the equation and explain it."},{t:"fdiv",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The formula card gives the rules for fractions as division and for multiplying fractions. Count this stop as supported, not independent."},{t:"order",std:["5.OA.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the order to work the grouping symbols in, so count these answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"vol",std:["5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The volume card gives the formula, the layer method, and the missing-height method. Count those items as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.2","W.5.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and list a source, so count the list of sources as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.5.9"],msg:"The frames remind the student to draw on each text, so rate on the content of the answer rather than on the structure."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote. Rate on the accuracy of the quote."},{t:"figs",std:["RL.5.4"],msg:"The card tells what a simile and a metaphor are, so count the simile and metaphor questions as supported."},{t:"tense",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],msg:"The verb tense card shows how to build each tense and lists the paired words. Count those items as supported."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"If the sources were read to the student, the notes still count for W.5.8, but write down that the sources were read aloud."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"Fraction strips show which common denominator lines the pieces up, so count the renaming step as supported, not independent."},{t:"fdiv",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"The formula card gives the rules for working with fractions. Count this stop as supported, not independent."},{t:"vol",std:["5.MD.C.3"],msg:"The volume card already says the answer is in cubic units, so rate on whether the student can explain what one cubic unit measures."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.1"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.2"],msg:"The frames tell the student to put it in short. Rate on whether the answer really is a summary and not a retelling."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the presentation, so count the sequencing check as supported, not independent."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
